<!--
    A stub that loads one of its own argument registers lost every argument
    the caller staged after that register.  An Objective-C image linked with
    -objc_stubs_fast sends each message through a __objc_stubs entry that
    loads the selector into x1 and jumps to objc_msgSend:

      objc_msgSend$countByEnumeratingWithState:objects:count:
                 adrp x1,...; ldr x1,[x1,#off]; adrp x16,...; ldr x16,[x16,#off]; br x16

    and the caller leaves x1 alone (the last call killed it) while it stages
    the real arguments in x2, x3 and x4:

      walk       mov x1,x19; bl prep; mov x0,x19; mov x2,sp; add x3,sp,#0x40;
                 mov w4,#0x10; bl objc_msgSend$countBy...

    x2, x3 and x4 score active, x1 scores definitely-not-used, and
    ParamListStandard::forceNoUse (fspec.cc:1623) reads the positional rule
    of the convention - the first register nobody fills ends the list - and
    turned the three active trials inactive, so the call printed
    `objc_msgSend$countBy...(a0)`: the address of the stack object never
    reached the call,
    the object's zero initialiser folded through it, and the loop read
    `*(unsigned long *)(v3 * 8)`.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 "active-input-trial-scoring"
    (kuna_calleefilledarg.rs, option calleefilledarg, default on).  At a
    call to an Objective-C selector stub, a register the stub's body writes
    on every path from its entry before anything could read it, that the
    caller never filled, is no parameter slot: the positional rule steps over
    it and the slots after it are scored on their own.  A C callee keeps the
    rule (kuna-calleefilledarg-ctramp.xml).

    Hand-written AArch64; `prep` is declared so its call kills x1 outright
    at the first send (at the second, inside the loop, x1 is a phi and the
    positional rule's hole filling applies either way).  walk_plain is the
    control: count_plain (`adrp x16; ldr x16; br x16`) does not fill x1, so
    the positional rule stands and the first call keeps one argument.
    `option calleefilledarg off` restores that rendering for walk.
-->
